L75: arXiv:2602.09992v1 [cs.CL] 10 Feb 2026
L76: # A Unified Assessment of the Poverty of the Stimulus Argument
L77: for Neural Language Models
L78: 
L79: Xiulin Yang Affiliation: Georgetown University    Arianna Bisazza Affiliation: University of Groningen {xy236, nathan.schneider, ethan.wilcox}@georgetown.edu  a.bisazza@rug.nl    Nathan Schneider Affiliation: Georgetown University    Ethan Gotlieb Wilcox Affiliation: Georgetown University
L80: ###### Abstract
L81: How can children acquire native-level syntax from limited input? According to the Poverty of the Stimulus Hypothesis (PoSH), the linguistic input children receive is insufficient to explain certain generalizations that are robustly learned; innate linguistic constraints, many have argued, are thus necessary to explain language learning. Neural language models, which lack such language-specific constraints in their design, offer a computational test of this longstanding (but controversial) claim.
L82: We introduce PoSH-Bench, a training-and-evaluation suite targeting question formation, islands to movement, and other English phenomena at the center of the PoSH arguments. Training Transformer models on 10–50M words of developmentally plausible text, we find indications of generalization on all phenomena even without direct positive evidence—yet neural models remain less data-efficient and their generalizations are weaker than those of children.
L83: We further enhance our models with three recently proposed cognitively motivated inductive biases. We find these biases improve general syntactic competence but not PoSH-Bench performance. Our findings challenge the claim that innate syntax is the only possible route to generalization, while suggesting that human-like data efficiency requires inductive biases beyond those tested here.
L84: ^{1}^{1} 1 Models: cite64†https://huggingface.co/collections/xiulinyang/posh-bench†huggingface.co ; Code: cite65†https://github.com/xiulinyang/posh-bench†github.com L85: ## 1 Introduction
L86: Figure 1: Performance of human vs. transformer learners with roughly the same amount of input, where model training sizes (10M, 30M) are aligned with the estimated cumulative input of children at specific developmental stages. Note: While differences in evaluation protocols between behavioral studies and model evaluations preclude direct quantitative comparison, the contrasting trajectories highlight that children acquire most of these phenomena with greater data efficiency within a limited scale.
L87: Children, despite substantial variation in their cultural and linguistic environments, still reliably acquire their native language within a limited developmental timeframe (cite66†Gleitman and Newport, 1995 , by age 5;). This robustness has long posed a central puzzle for theories of language acquisition and remains a topic of active debate within the field. For decades, such observations have motivated the argument from Poverty of the Stimulus (PoS) for nativism (cite67†Chomsky, 1965 ).
L88: The logic is straightforward: if the input is too sparse to distinguish the correct generalization rule from all other plausible alternatives, learners must possess linguistic-specific biases that guide them toward the correct hypothesis (cite68†Chomsky and others, 1976 ). This argument draws upon various linguistic phenomena, such as syntactic islands, where children reject unconstrained movement despite input that superficially licenses such generalizations.
L89: Such nativist claims now face an empirical challenge from neural language models. Modern Transformer models acquire impressive syntactic competence from text alone, passing various grammaticality tests (cite69†Warstadt et al., 2020 ; cite70†Wilcox et al., 2024 ) without explicit linguistic constraints.
L90: Critics, however, point out that neural models typically train on billions of words—orders of magnitude more than a child encounters by age five ($\approx$10–50M words; cite71†Warstadt and Bowman 2022 ; cite72†Frank 2023 ). Perhaps the stimulus is indeed impoverished at child scale, and neural models succeed only by compensating with massive data.
L91: This raises our central question: Can transformers reliably learn PoS phenomena when trained on input matching the quantity and quality of children’s linguistic experience?^{2}^{2} 2 Human learners acquire language in rich multimodal environments. Due to the limitation of current resources for cognitively plausible multimodal data, we limit our experiments to text input only.
L92: We are not alone in posing this question; indeed, many recent efforts have attempted to answer this question empirically. A growing body of work, including recent BabyLM Challenges (cite73†Warstadt et al., 2023 ; cite74†Hu et al., 2024 ; cite75†Charpentier et al., 2025 ), trained language models on controlled input at human-scale data sizes and evaluated using paradigms inspired by developmental research (cite76†Yedetore et al., 2023 ; cite77†Evanson et al., 2023 , e.g.,).
L93: However, three critical gaps limit our ability to use these models as valid tests of the PoS argument.
L94: First, existing work exhibits a mismatch between developmentally plausible training conditions and systematic targeted evaluation on PoS phenomena. Human acquisition research centers on early childhood (ages 3–5), corresponding to approximately 10–50M words of exposure. However, computational models are typically trained on cognitively plausible data that fall outside this window—ranging from limited scales ($\leq$10M words) to larger 100M-word datasets (cite73†Warstadt et al., 2023 , e.g.,).
L95: This dichotomy leaves the critical 10–50M word range, which aligns most closely with the Poverty of the Stimulus acquisition period, underexplored. Furthermore, existing benchmarks (e.g., BLiMP (cite69†Warstadt et al., 2020 ), Zorro (cite78†Huebner et al., 2021 ), SCaMP (cite79†McCoy and Griffiths, 2025 )) assess broad linguistic generalization but do not systematically target canonical PoS phenomena. Even PoS-focused studies often examine individual phenomena in isolation, limiting cross-phenomenon comparison.
L96: Bridging this gap thus requires a unified framework that evaluates learners within the 10–50M word window against a dedicated PoS benchmark.
L97: Second, the role of indirect evidence in acquiring PoS phenomena remains untested under developmentally plausible conditions. The PoS argument rests on the premise that (positive) direct evidence is too sparse to support generalization, necessitating innate constraints (cite80†Yang, 2016 ; cite81†Legate and Yang, 2002 ).
L98: Recent computational work challenges this, suggesting that indirect evidence may be richer than previously assumed (cite82†Misra and Mahowald, 2024 ; cite83†Patil et al., 2024 ; cite84†Yao et al., 2025 ; cite85†Foraker et al., 2009 ). However, these studies usually assess models using adult-directed text (cite83†Patil et al., 2024 , e.g.,) or do not target canonical PoS phenomena.
L99: To rigorously test whether the stimulus is truly impoverished, we must test at more granular data scales and explicitly manipulate the availability of direct positive evidence and evaluate whether models can generalize in its absence.
L100: Third, the inductive biases that facilitate PoS generalization remain unclear. While prior work has proposed and validated potential structural biases that improve linguistic competence overall (cite86†Hu et al., 2025 ; cite87†Yoshida et al., 2024 , e.g.,), we lack systematic evidence about (i) what sorts of biases facilitate PoS generalization, specifically, and (ii) whether biases that improve general syntactic competence also help with PoS-specific learning.
L101: This is crucial for understanding what kinds of innate constraints, if any, are needed.
L102: To address these gaps, we introduce PoSH-Bench (P overty o f the S timulus H ypothesis Bench mark), a suite designed to combine canonical PoS phenomena (islands, question formation, binding, and wanna) and child-scale input with the explicit manipulation of direct positive evidence.
L103: We use PoSH-Bench to answer the following three questions: (1) To what extent can transformer models acquire PoS phenomena from developmentally plausible input? (2) How do input type, data scale, and direct positive evidence affect learnability? (3) Do certain cognitively grounded inductive biases facilitate such learning?
L104: Our findings show that models achieve above-chance generalization on all four PoS phenomena with from as little as 10M words without direct positive evidence, suggesting that indirect evidence is richer than PoS arguments have traditionally assumed (cite28†Section 5 ). However, a persistent learning efficiency gap (cite88†Figure 1 ) reveals that models remain substantially less data-efficient than children overall (cite31†Section 6 ).
L105: Moreover, we experiment with three types of inductive biases, two linguistic-specific and one domain-general, and we find that while these cognitive biases improve general syntactic competence, crucially, they fail to close the efficiency gap for the phenomena targeted in PoSH-Bench, suggesting that human-like acquisition requires mechanisms beyond both distributional learning and the structural constraints explored here (cite34†Section 7 ).
L106: ## 2 Background & Related Work
L107: ### 2.1 The Heart of the Learnability Debate
L108: Children acquire complex linguistic knowledge from limited data that could, in principle, support multiple abstract grammatical hypotheses. Yet they consistently converge on a single (correct) generalization. For example, parents do not explicitly tell children that a noun phrase cannot be extracted from an adjunct clause (*Who did he trust Jack after seeing?), yet children reliably acquire this constraint.
L109: Broadly speaking, two theoretical approaches, nativism and empiricism, offer competing explanations (cite89†Pearl, 2022 ): does syntax acquisition require innate, domain-specific constraints, or is the input itself sufficiently informative for the correct generalizations to be learned from broader domain-general mechanisms?
L110: Nativist approaches distinguish four kinds of evidence based on two dimensions: positive vs. negative and direct vs. indirect (cite89†Pearl, 2022 ). Positive evidence signals which forms are grammatical, while negative evidence signals which are ungrammatical. Direct evidence explicitly targets the correct hypothesis; indirect evidence requires inference from context or co-occurrence patterns.
L111: Although many linguistic phenomena can be acquired from direct evidence, others lack such evidence yet are nevertheless acquired by children.
L112: For these phenomena, the absence of direct negative evidence (e.g., explicit correction) (cite90†Brown, 1970 ; cite91†Bowerman, 1988 ; cite81†Legate and Yang, 2002 , inter alia) and the sparsity of direct positive evidence (cite81†Legate and Yang, 2002 ) forces learners to rely primarily on indirect positive input that is compatible with multiple hypotheses, giving rise to the poverty of the stimulus.
L113: Empiricism, in contrast, argues that the input is sufficiently rich when processed by powerful, domain-general mechanisms such as distributional learning (cite92†Aslin and Newport, 2012 ) and pragmatic inference (cite93†Ambridge and Lieven, 2011 ). Under this view, linguistic structures are learned from the data with general learning capabilities rather than from language-specific innate constraints.
L114: Following the linguistic nativist tradition, the PoS argument (cite67†Chomsky, 1965 ) can be formally characterized as follows, drawing on cite89†Pearl (2022) ; cite94†Pullum and Scholz (2002) ; cite95†Perfors et al. (2011) .
