L166: Table 2: Word counts (in millions) by data source and total target size for each training dataset. Figure 2: Poverty of the Stimulus Category-wise average for each benchmark. The horizontal dashed line represents chance-level performance. The shadow/error bar represents SD across 3 random seeds.
L167: Building on this base corpus (baby-base; see cite140†Table 2 ), we construct three training conditions. To isolate the effect of direct positive evidence while controlling for corpus size, we create two sentence-matched variants of the child-directed corpus.^{6}^{6} 6 Detailed statistics are provided in cite47†Appendix B .
L168: baby-f: A filtered version of baby-base with all direct positive evidence sentences for the four target phenomena (cite107†Table 1 ) removed, simulating a learner exposed only to indirect evidence. Filtering details and sanity check in cite48†Appendix C .
L169: baby: Created from baby-f by replacing a small proportion of sentences with direct positive evidence examples ($\approx$0.2% for question formation; $\approx$0.07% for binding), maintaining identical sentence count while reintroducing the sparse direct evidence characteristic of children’s natural input. We construct both baby-f and baby only for 10–50M scales, as the 100M condition exceeds developmentally plausible early acquisition, where the PoS argument is most relevant.
L170: For 100M, we use only the unfiltered baby-base condition which we refer to as baby in this condition for simplicity. wiki: Wikipedia-based corpora of matched word counts, providing adult-directed baselines with denser syntax and more complex vocabulary.
L171: ### 3.2 Evaluation Suite & Metrics
L172: PoSH-Bench covers four major categories discussed in cite9†Section 2.2 (Question Formation, Binding, Islands, and Wanna), spanning nine subcategories drawn from the acquisition literature (see examples in cite107†Table 1 ). Each subcategory includes 500 manually verified minimal pairs to ensure both syntactic contrast and semantic plausibility. Pairs are generated templatically.
L173: The templates used to create minimal pairs can be found in cite55†Appendix F and special considerations for each subcategory can be found in cite56†Appendix G .
L174: Three of our PoSH phenomena are also represented in various existing benchmarks (see details in cite141†Table 11 ). However, PoSH-Bench differs from these previous studies in that it allows a systematic examination on PoS phenomena simultaneously under developmentally plausible settings. To enable fair comparison across input conditions (wiki vs.
L175: baby), all lexical items are sampled from the intersection of the top 3K most frequent words shared between the wiki-100m and baby-100m corpora, giving 1,950 word types (including punctuations).
L176: ## 4 Method: Training and Evaluation
L177: ### 4.1 Models and Training
L178: All models adopt the GPT-2 architecture (cite142†Radford et al., 2019 ), a standard transformer language model with an autoregressive training objective. For each dataset split, we train a separate tokenizer. This is important because although the wiki-100m and baby-100m corpora contain a comparable number of words, different vocabulary sizes yield substantially different token counts after tokenization.
L179: We therefore experiment with five common vocabulary sizes and select $32{,}768$, which minimizes compression-rate discrepancies across datasets (see cite143†Table 9 in cite53†Appendix D ).
L180: A pilot study comparing model capacities shows that gpt2-mini achieves the lowest dev-set loss on 10M-scale data, whereas gpt2-small performs better on larger datasets. Accordingly, we use gpt2-mini for 10M experiments and gpt2-small for 30M, 50M, and 100M, training each configuration with three random seeds. Models are trained for up to 100k steps with early stopping (patience = 6k) and a 4k-step linear warmup (cite144†Padovani et al., 2025 ). Full details are provided in cite54†Appendix E .
L181: ### 4.2 Evaluation
L182: 
L183: For each minimal pair in PoSH-Bench, we compute sentence-level perplexity for both sentences and count an item as correct if the grammatical sentence receives lower perplexity. Accuracy (the proportion of correct preferences), averaged across three seeds, serves as the primary metric. Our implementation builds on minicons (cite145†Misra, 2022 ).
